    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Faults below the limit of seismic resolution in reservoirs can be important, particularly in the way that they control the flow of hydrocarbons during production, and yet they are difficult to detect. The size distribution of subseismic faults can be predicted by extrapolating the size distribution measured at the seismic scale down to the subseismic scale. However, the positions and orientations of the subseismic faults are more difficult to determine.\r\n\r\nPoly_3D was utilized in an attempt to predict the positions and orientations of subseismic faults in the rock volume in the Oseberg Syd field, northern North Sea. The large, seismically resolvable faults were brought into a 3D numerical mechanical model to determine the stress conditions near these faults at the time of faulting. The stress field was then combined with Coulomb failure criteria to predict the orientations and densities of the smaller faults using information from only the large faults. The result- ing simulations compared well with the known faulting.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
